bn254 — provenance note
========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bls12_381`/
`bip340`/`adaptor` modules — self-contained crypto modules not folded into
the root `NOTICE`). Parts 1-3 (field tower, `G1`/`G2`) needed no such file
— see `SPEC.md`'s original "NOTICE — why no entry" reasoning (EIP-196/197
is a public spec, and the field/curve constants are mathematical facts,
not copyrightable third-party expression). Part 4's KAT harness changes
that: it uses a third-party library's COMPUTED OUTPUT (not merely its
published constant VALUES) as a numeric oracle, the same posture
`bls12_381/NOTICE` documents for its own `e(G1,G2)` cross-check — hence
this file.

Provenance
----------

1. **EIP-196 / EIP-197** (Ethereum precompile specifications,
   https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-196 /
   https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-197) — the source of truth for
   BN254's field modulus, curve equation, generator points, and the
   `G1`/`G2` wire-encoding conventions (Parts 1-3). Public specifications
   — not copyrightable (merger doctrine, `CONVENTIONS.md` §5).
2. **`ethereum/py_ecc`** (MIT license,
   https://github.com/ethereum/py_ecc) — a DESIGN/FACT reference for
   Parts 1-3 (its published `field_modulus`/`curve_order`/`G1`/`G2`
   constant VALUES were cross-checked, not its source read or ported —
   see `SPEC.md`'s "Model-after + seed"), AND, newly for Part 4, a
   NUMERIC ORACLE: `py_ecc.bn128.bn128_pairing.pairing` (installed from
   PyPI, 2026-07-15) was run to independently compute `e(G1,G2)`,
   `e([2]G1,G2)`, and `e([3]G1,G2)` for `pairing.zig`'s pinned KAT
   vectors. `py_ecc.bn128`'s SOURCE (`bn128_curve.py`/`bn128_pairing.py`,
   both MIT-licensed) was read only as a FACT source about `py_ecc`'s own
   `Fp12` representation and loop-parameter convention (its flat
   `Fp[w]/(w^12-18w^6+82)` basis, and its `ate_loop_count = 6x+2`, a
   POSITIVE loop parameter needing no sign correction — unlike
   `bls12_381`'s py_ecc cross-check, which needed a documented
   conjugation for BLS12-381's NEGATIVE seed) — NOT as a design/algorithm
   source for this module's OWN `pairing.zig` doc comments, which cite
   Devegili-Scott-Dahab (2007) and Fuentes-Castañeda-Knapp-
   Rodríguez-Henríquez (2011) directly (see `SPEC.md`'s "Part 4 —
   pairing").
3. **The `py_ecc`-to-this-module tower basis conversion** (`SPEC.md`'s
   "Part 4 — KAT generation" has the full derivation) — an
   INDEPENDENTLY-DERIVED Fp-linear bijection between `py_ecc`'s flat
   `Fp12` basis and this module's own `Fp2`/`Fp6`/`Fp12` tower (both
   share the relation `w^6 = ξ = 9+u` by construction), validated as a
   ring homomorphism on 20 random `Fp12` products (using a from-scratch
   Python re-implementation of THIS module's own tower-multiplication
   formulas, already established by `SPEC.md`'s §4 field-tower KAT
   generator) before being trusted to convert `py_ecc`'s pairing outputs
   into `pairing.zig`'s pinned hex vectors. This conversion is this
   module's OWN work product (a linear-algebra derivation over two
   already-independently-verified field-tower constructions), not
   ported from any third party.
4. **Scott, Benger, Charlemagne, Perez, Kachisa, "On the Final
   Exponentiation for Calculating Pairings on Ordinary Elliptic Curves"**
   (ePrint 2008/490), **Devegili, Scott, Dahab, "Implementing
   Cryptographic Pairings over Barreto-Naehrig Curves"** (Pairing 2007),
   and **Fuentes-Castañeda, Knapp, Rodríguez-Henríquez, "Faster Hashing
   to G2"** (SAC 2011) — the academic references `pairing.zig`'s
   `millerLoop`/`finalExponentiation` doc comments cite for the exact
   `6x+2` Miller loop / Frobenius-tail / BN-specific exact-`d` hard-part
   addition chain. Cited for attribution courtesy (public academic
   papers) — the ALGORITHMS were implemented from these papers' published
   constructions, not by porting any third-party source (see the closing
   note below). The Fuentes-Castañeda variant is named as the one the
   pass deliberately did NOT use (it computes a fixed power of `f^d`,
   byte-different from the exact-`d` chain the KAT pins).

Verification performed (Part 4 KAT-generation + crypto-core passes, 2026-07-15)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- **The `bn_ate_loop_param` constant**: confirmed equal to `py_ecc.bn128
  .bn128_pairing.ate_loop_count` (`29793968203157093288`) both by direct
  comparison against the installed library's constant AND by independent
  arithmetic (`6*4965661367192848881+2`, computed outside `py_ecc` too).
- **The basis-conversion ring homomorphism**: `map(A)*map(B) ==
  map(A*B)` (under `py_ecc`'s own `FQ12.__mul__`) checked on 20 random
  `Fp12` element pairs, using a from-scratch Python tower-multiplication
  implementation (this module's own §4 KAT-generator formulas, NOT
  `py_ecc`'s arithmetic) on one side and `py_ecc`'s arithmetic on the
  other — an homomorphism check between two INDEPENDENT implementations,
  not a self-consistency tautology. Also confirmed `map(w_tower)^6 ==
  map(ξ_tower)` (the defining relation survives the map) and that
  `py_ecc.bn128.G1`/`G2` are byte-identical to this module's own
  `g1.zig`/`g2.zig` generators before computing any pairing value.
- **Bilinearity sanity on the ORACLE itself** (derived independently of
  this module's own implementation, before it was trusted): `py_ecc
  .bn128.pairing(G2, [2]G1) == py_ecc.bn128.pairing([2]G2, G1)`, and both equal
  `py_ecc.bn128.pairing(G2, G1)**2`; similarly `pairing(G2, [3]G1) ==
  pairing(G2, G1)**3`. Also `e(G1,G2) != 1` and `e(G1,G2)**r == 1`. All
  four checked directly in Python before any value was pinned as a Zig
  KAT constant.

No SOURCE from `py_ecc` (or `zkcrypto`/`gnark`/`libff`, all named only as
"the same shape every serious implementation follows" design references
in `pairing.zig`'s doc comments and otherwise UNREAD) was ported into this
module. `pairing.zig`'s two crypto cores (`millerLoop`,
`finalExponentiation`) were implemented from the published academic
constructions above (item 4), then verified byte-exact against `py_ecc`'s
computed pairing outputs via the KAT harness this NOTICE documents (see
`SPEC.md`'s "Part 4 — pairing", "Part 4 — KAT generation", and
"Backlog").

